\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to weaken crown.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Laca   \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2004\par 15:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, July 8 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up its fight against the firming of the crown on Thursday with several waves of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The monetary body has been fighting against crown appreciation for over a year, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance was too quick and that excessive gains could be dangerous for the small open economy that entered the European Union in May.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said the rally is fuelled by hot-money inflows, but analysts said the main reason behind the rise were Slovakia's solid economic fundamentals, which would drive the unit to stronger levels again over the longer term.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market, it was buying (euros) in waves... I would say they were buying in a range and that the top of the action was at around 39.900," one dealer at a foreign bank unit in Bratislava said.   \par \par \uc2 The crown firmed through a psychological level of 39.700 per euro at around midday on buying by a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 Some traders said they saw the crown's all-time high at 39.666 per euro, but one foreign bank said that rate was an off-market price and the real record high where deals were made was 39.675.   \par \par \uc2 Traders estimated the bank might have bought 150-200 million euros in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were not immediately available for comment.   \par \par \uc2 Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first direct action on the market since May last year, came after a series of verbal warnings that the bank would use all its tools to prevent unjustified crown jumps.   \par \par \uc2 The bank has cut rates by 150 basis points in three moves this year to make the crown less attractive for carry players taking advantage of higher interest rates compared with other markets. The two key week-repo rate is now 4.50 percent.   \par \par \uc2 CROWN DOWN, SEEN RISING AGAIN   \par \par \uc2 The direct market action weakened the crown to around 39.950 per euro in afternoon trading, and analysts expected investors to become more cautious in the next few days.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is typically designed to slow the pace of crown appreciation rather than reverse the trend. They may try and drive (euro/crown) up to 40.10, but after that they'll probably step back," said Koon Chow, emerging markets strategist at CSFB.   \par \par \uc2 "Equally the market may wait a bit before creeping back into long positions."   \par \par \uc2 However, dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might only slow the crown firming because it was based on Slovakia's strong economic growth - at 5.5 percent in the first quarter - improving trade balance and expectations of foreign direct investment projects.   \par \par \uc2 "This halts the firming for some time. It will not change the trend, and we might see new highs again, but it will not be as quick as recently," said ING Bank trader Jan Rafaj. (Additional reporting by James Thornhill in London).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040708e078001k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
