\par\par \b \uc2 Slovak crown jumps to 5-1/2 mo highs, eyes cbank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2003\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par   jumped to five-and-half month highs against the euro on\par Thursday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comments eased market worries about\par a near-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the local unit, dealers said.\par     The crown ended at 41.020/080 to the euro, its main\par reference unit, after 41.100/160 late on Wednesday. It firmed to\par a three-week high of 35.051/111 to the dollar, from 35.336/379.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Marian Jusko reiterated on Thursday\par the bank's position that the crown should firm only in the long\par term and the move had to be justified by sound economic\par fundamentals.\par  Although it was not viewed as a policy shift, dealers said\par the comments appeared to have partially lifted players' fears\par the bank would take aggressive measures to prevent gradual crown\par strengthening beyond 41.000 to the euro.\par     "It does not look from the comments that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par would like to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dramatically at the moment...\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  it looks\par we can get under 41.000 per euro level in the near future," CSOB\par dealer Robert Hakszer said.\par     The crown touched the 41.030 per euro level during the\par session, nearing sensitive levels last seen in May when the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  knocked the unit off its all-time highs of 40.800\par per euro.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's long-term expectations were reflected in\par an immediate change of the exchange rate. It was an impulse\par mainly for foreign players to buy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crowns," Hakszer said.\par     The bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly in 2003 to prevent excessive\par firming of the crown due worries that a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could\par hurt exporters. It also cut interest rates by 25 basis point in\par September to show it might use this tool to fend off\par speculators.\par     But traders said the crown would probably not stay under\par 41.000 per euro for long as uncertainty in the market might\par increase when parliament opens debate on the 2004 budget draft.\par     On the secondary bond market, the yield on the benchmark\par five-year bonds  shrank to 5.329/195 percent from\par 5.400/200, and the 10-year paper moved to 5.413/343 percent from\par 5.500/300 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031113dzbd003cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
