\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Slovak April trade gap worse than market forecasts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Laca \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2003\par 11:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, May 29 (Reuters) - Slovakia's foreign trade deficit broke a six-month narrowing trend in April and widened on the year as imports outpaced slowing exports for the first time since October, data showed on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The trade deficit of the future EU member's small, open economy grew to 6.985 billion crowns ($200.5 million) in April, its widest level this year, from a gap of 5.495 billion in the same month of 2002, the statistics office said. \par \par \uc2  The gap was bigger than market expectations for a 2.9 billion-crown shortfall. \par \par \uc2  "It was a surprise to us. We expected a much better number, and much more benign import figures due to (a fall in) the price of oil," said ING Bank chief economist Jan Toth. \par \par \uc2  The trade figures pushed the crown weaker, and analysts said the data, coupled with an absence of firming pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had relieved pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates at a policy meet on Friday. \par \par \uc2  A key reason behind the wider deficit was a deceleration in export growth, which slowed to 11.4 percent year-on-year in April from 24.4 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the smaller export rise was mainly due to a high comparative base from last year, and that none of Slovakia's major export categories had underperformed in April. \par \par \uc2  Commerzbank analyst Radomir Jac said the base effect would play a significant role in the trade balance for most of the second half of 2003, but that soft domestic demand should still help narrow the full-year shortfall. \par \par \uc2  Imports were above analysts' expectations, as the predicted impact of low crude prices following the war in Iraq and the weak dollar had not shown up in Slovakia's April oil bill. \par \par \uc2  Imports grew 12.9 percent on the year, from 14.8 percent in the previous month, and the cumulative four-month trade gap totalled 13.546 billion crowns, down from 24.884 billion in the same period of 2002. \par \par \uc2  CROWN DOWN, RATES SEEN FLAT \par \par \uc2  The market had expected the data to provide room for a new inflow of foreign capital after a month of stagnation following a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that knocked the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off record highs in early May. \par \par \uc2  The crown, which has become an investor favourite among central European EU candidates due to its booming economy and high interest rates, weakened to 41.120/135 to the euro after the trade data, from 41.000/089 at the open. \par \par \uc2  Tatra Bank analysts Robert Prega said the wider trade deficit would probably reinforce the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance of fighting against crown firming. The bank is worried that too strong a crown will hit exporters and further deepen the external imbalance. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly warned it could cut interest rates - the main two-week repo rate is now at 6.5 percent - to prevent hot money inflows, but analysts now see no imminent policy easing as the bank does not need to respond to speculative firming pressure. \par \par \uc2  "The likelihood of an interest rate cut is very low at tomorrow's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, and I think there will be a correction of expectations on the money market as well," said CSOB Bank analyst Marek Gabris. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030529dz5t00fa9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
