\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak cbank bought 50 mln euro from banks in Dec. \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2003\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 31 (Reuters) - The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , battling to dampen demand for the local crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Friday it had bought 50 million euros from commercial banks in December. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few months of 2002 as speculative capital inflows, attracted by high interest rates, pushed the crown to record highs against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bank, worried that too strong a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hit export comptitiveness and thus the country's trade balance, also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  both on the open market and in discreet deals with banks in November to take the steam out of the crown's advance. \par \par \uc2  Individual deals with banks continued in December, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary report said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks may approach the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they have large orders to avoid artificial influences on Slovakia's small \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  After a retreat at the end of last year, the crown resumed its rise early in 2003 and the bank repeatedly warned it would use all its tools - \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate cuts - to prevent excessive gains. However, the crown fell sharply last week as speculators fled central Europe following heavy losses in Hungary when that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cut rates to knock the forint down. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit has stabilised this week, trading between 41.600-41.900 per euro, its key reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the crown to appreciate further this year as the country's economy converges with the European Union, which Slovakia will join in 2004. \par \par \uc2  A crown advance could prompt the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rate by 50 to 100 basis points within the next six months in order to discourage speculators, they said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030131dz1v00qs1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
