\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-Slovak cbank sees long fight with speculators. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Winfrey \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2003\par 17:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) Governor Marian Jusko said on Wednesday the bank would repeat drastic moves like the major cuts to interest rates it made in November if forced to do so by unjustified firming in the crown. \par \par \uc2  Jusko's comments coincided with a two-percent drop in the crown on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  It has been hammered this week after Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fended off speculative capital causing unwanted firming in the forint with huge interest rate cuts and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed interest rates by 150 basis points and bought around half a billion euros on the market to knock the crown lower after it hit all time highs of 41.100 against the single European unit. \par \par \uc2  "If the situation calls for drastic measures similar to those made in November, yes, the national bank will take these measures... We will not hesitate," Jusko said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "Artificial strengthening in the Slovak crown pushed only by expectations would be harmful to the future development of the economy, and this factor is the most important thing in our present situation." \par \par \uc2  The central banks in Slovakia, the Czech Republic, Poland and Hungary have fought firming pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last year due to speculative plays by foreign investors hoping for fat returns as the countries near EU entry in 2004 and adoption of the euro, expected around four years later. \par \par \uc2  Jusko said the recent steps taken by his Hungarian counterparts, including slashing their key interest rates by 200 basis points over two days, were necessary to counter possible negative economic effects. \par \par \uc2  He said his and other central European central banks would likely have to continue fighting speculators until the region completes eurozone entry. \par \par \uc2  "I think it will last for the whole period, from now up until our entry into the EMU... The action by the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was really very very drastic, but it was necessary as the forint was becoming stronger and stronger, and perhaps it didn't correspond to the fundamentals of the economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  "To have too strong a forint, or too strong a crown, could be hard for the further developments of our economies." \par \par \uc2  SEES CROWN GRADUALLY FIRMING \par \par \uc2  Jusko said Slovakia should join EMU before or at the same time as its neighbours in the region, but not after. \par \par \uc2  And if met, forecasts of increased exports, better state fiscal performance and heady growth should also allow the crown to firm gradually before Slovakia enters the ERM-2 exchange rate mechanism it must follow for two years before eurozone entry. \par \par \uc2  "It seems to me that the first possible date that Slovakia could enter the EMU will be 2008... And if our expectations materialise, the crown should be stronger," Jusko said. \par \par \uc2  The bank sees GDP up by four percent or more annually through 2006. \par \par \uc2  Slovakia's invitations to join the EU and NATO, and the election of a centre-right government that has pledged to reform state finances and cut spending are all positive economic factors, but the NBS will not allow market expectations to push the crown to a point outpacing real economic developments, Jusko said. \par \par \uc2  He added he expected December monthly and 2002 full-year foreign trade data, due out next week, to show improvements over 2001, while the troublesome foreign trade gap should also show an improvement this year on the back of lower domestic demand from the government's measures and higher exports. \par \par \uc2  Jusko said the bank would probably need several months to analyse the effects of the government's austerity measures and the potential improvement in foreign trade, before it judged whether a new trend had been established. He would not comment on the bank's possible future policy towards interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "We need to analyse the situation for two or three months at a minimum because the new (austerity) measures of the government have only just come into effect in January," Jusko said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030122dz1m00vhz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
