\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak car plant news boosts crown for second day. \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2003\par 17:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par   end higher against both the euro and the dollar on\par Thursday, continuing to benefit from news of a new car factory\par to be built in the EU designate member, but dealers said the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bias against a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may limit gains.\par     The crown retreated slightly from its best level of the day\par after some late profit-taking but still closed at 41.290/350\par against its key reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro, from 41.360/380\par the previous session.\par     It also closed at a fresh four-year high against the dollar\par at 39.040/100 after an intraday best of around 38.90 bid and\par compared with Wednesday's finish at 39.100 in the middle.\par     The crown jumped three-quarters-of-a-percent on Wednesday on\par market anticipation of foreign capital inflows, after PSA\par Peugeot Citroen's (PEUP.PA) said it would build a 700 million\par euro plant in Slovakia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "The move yesterday was mostly due to the PSA deal, and it\par continued today until there was some profit taking below 41.200\par by foreign banks. I see a range of 41.200-41.400 for the near\par future, but the mood still favours strengthening in the crown,"\par said CSOB dealer Richard Brza.\par     He added that foreign banks continued to sell euro for\par crowns, and that the Slovak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also was supported by gains\par by the Hungarian and Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Hungarian forint        flirted with the top of its 30\par percent trading band on Thursday, prompting the Hungarian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut its base rate by 100 basis points in order\par to cool demand for the unit which like in other EU designate\par countries, has benefited from foreign investment inflows.\par     Dealers were wary of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which verbally\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday, saying the PSA announcement, while\par positive for the economy, had not changed the current\par fundamentals of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economy, and that the bank would\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market or slash interest rates if the crown\par experienced unjustifiable firming.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030116dz1g00w3s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
