\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Slovak November trade gap lower than expected. \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2003\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Jan 3 (Reuters) - Slovakia's foreign trade deficit surprisingly narrowed in November 2002 on the back of renewed strong export growth and cheaper fuel imports, data showed on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The November trade shortfall totalled 9.737 billion crowns ($246 million), down from a 12.781 billion crown gap in the same month of 2001, the Slovak Statistics Office said. \par \par \uc2  The deficit was better than market expectations as a Reuters survey of analysts last month showed an average forecast for a 12.6 billion crown deficit in November. \par \par \uc2  "The data are better than expected.... What was surprising is the solid growth of exports after (a) worse performance in the previous few months," said Commerzbank analyst Radomir Jac. \par \par \uc2  Exports rose 11.3 percent year-on-year in November, outpacing a 4.5 percent annual rise in imports. \par \par \uc2  The statistics office also revised October monthly trade data to show a gap of 13.152 billion crowns, from an originally reported 13.003 billion. \par \par \uc2  The cumulative trade deficit for January-November of 2002 totalled 83.412 billion crowns, compared with 86.828 billion in the same period a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the better-than-predicted trade balance was also partially affected by imports of fossil fuels, mainly Russian crude oil and natural gas, which were cheaper due to the strength of the Slovak crown against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Raw materials, the second largest import category, totalled 99.163 billion crowns in the first 11 months of last year, down 8.4 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the improving trade balance would mean upward pressure on the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which rose immediately after the release of November trade data. \par \par \uc2  The crown traded at 41.230/260 to the euro, its key reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as of 0830 GMT, from 41.335/395 at Thursday's close. It was hovering near four-year highs against the dollar at 39.799/838. \par \par \uc2  "The trade development is positive for the crown... We can expect pressure on strengthening, but, at the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to act against the firming because it would probably still find it undesirable," Jac said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last year to attempt to stop the crown's rise, which it said was unjustified due to the trade balance deficit. ($1=39.51 Slovak crowns). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030103dz1300gtl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
