\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank bought EUR583mln in Nov  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2002\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Dec 23 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it had bought 583 million euros from commercial banks in total through discreet and direct deals in November, to halt the rise of the crown. \par \par \uc2  The bank's monthly monetary survey said it bought 55 million euros from commercial banks discreetly in individual deals at the beginning of the month, and the rest of the entire amount in direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in three days beginning on November 12. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were followed by a surprising 150-basis-point cut in key interest rates effective from November 18, aimed at lowering the interest rate differential with the euro zone and neighbouring markets and thus narrowing the room for speculation. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown's jump was sparked by the victory of centre-right, pro-reform parties in September elections. The crown rose by around one percent in October to 41.577 to the euro, its key reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank bought 30 million euros in discreet deals in October when it began to fight the unit's firming. \par \par \uc2  However, the crown continued to firm in November, hitting an all-time high of 41.100 early in that month, as foreign funds poured into central European markets in the expectation of gains once the region joins the eurozone in around 2008. \par \par \uc2  The bank said it had started to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly at the crown's level of around 41.190 per euro, and that the moves resulted in the unit's easing by 1.4 percent versus the euro and by 1.3 percent to the dollar over the month. \par \par \uc2  This month the crown has continued to ease due to a typical end-year mood, and is seen trading within the 41.750-950 per euro range this week. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021223dycn00mp2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
