\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak crown ends slightly lower, trade thin. \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2002\par 17:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par  closed marginally lower on Friday in quiet trade and\par dealers said it may have room to ease further in the near term\par due to profit taking and the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The crown has also shown little immediate reaction to news\par that Slovakia and six other ex-communist states had been invited\par to join NATO at the Alliance's summit in Prague on Thursday.\par     The crown was at 41.544/594 to its key reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par the euro, from 41.510/550 late on Thursday. It was at 41.570/663\par to the dollar, from 41.420/460.\par     "It was pretty stable today... I don't expect strengthening\par below 41.400. There was no impact from the NATO invitation\par because it was already priced in," said Slovenska Sporitelna\par dealer Juraj Zabadal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Last week the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market selling crowns for euros and also cut its key\par interest rate by 150 basis points to 6.5 percent in an effort to\par stem demand for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The crown has strengthened around seven percent since July\par on the back of a win by reformist forces in September elections\par and a generally bullish mood towards central Europe as Slovakia\par and other EU candidates approach the planned 2004 accession date\par and euro adoption a few years later.\par     But Zabadal said that London banks had created new positions\par during the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign and were selling\par crowns at around 41.400 to the euro, so that the unit may not\par strengthen past that point in the near term.\par     He also said some players were wary of further action from\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and noted that crown gains could be limited by\par traditional year-end easing, which usually occurs in December as\par foreign firms repatriate profits.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021122dybm00uql\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
