\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak crown slips on foreign profit taking. \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2002\par 17:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 21 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par  closed slightly lower versus the euro on Thursday.\par     It failed to react to news that Slovakia had been invited to\par join NATO and was pulled down mainly by foreign profit taking.\par     The crown closed at 41.510/550 to its key reference\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro, from 41.460/520 late on Wednesday. It was at\par 41.420/460 to the dollar, from 41.290/350.\par     Dealers said that the market did not react at all to news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  that Slovakia and six other central European countries were \par \par \uc2  invited to join NATO in around 2004, as this had been widely \par \par \uc2  expected for some time. \par \par \uc2  "We saw a slight upward drive in the morning, but then \par \par \uc2  foreign players started to buy (foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), pulling the \par \par \uc2  crown lower," said Slovenska Sporitelna dealer Vladimir Gajdos. \par \par \uc2  "When the crown was already at around 41.470 to the euro, \par \par \uc2  one London bank bought a relatively large volume (of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) from the market which was pretty thin and the unit \par \par \uc2  fell to the closing levels," Gajdos said. \par \par \uc2  He added he saw the crown trading between 41.400 and 41.600 \par \par \uc2  to the euro on Friday as both the levels are strongly supported \par \par \uc2  by bids and offers, respectively. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the crown, which has gained around seven percent \par \par \uc2  since July, has the potential to firm further, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around all-time highs levels hit last week to \par \par \uc2  knock it lower. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fighting off hot-money inflows into the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown and other central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  three times to sell crowns and slashed interest rates by 150 \par \par \uc2  basis points last week. \par \par \uc2  But the unit resumed its gradual firmer trend this week. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be able to slow its \par \par \uc2  rise with further rate cuts and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that over the \par \par \uc2  long term, it may not be able to stop the tide. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaffirmed its resolve to keep the crown \par \par \uc2  weaker on Wednesday, saying it would use all of its tools to \par \par \uc2  fend off capital inflows that have boosted the crown for reasons \par \par \uc2  not justified by macroeconomic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But bank Governor Marian Jusko added that an expected rise \par \par \uc2  in inflation next year may limit the room for more cuts. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021121dybl00w44\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
