\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  crown firms despite cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2002\par 17:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crown\par  closed stronger on Thursday despite a third straight day\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken it, and dealers said\par foreign buying will likely keep the unit under firming pressure.\par     The crown was at 41.600/650 to the euro, its key reference\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at the end of the session, compared with 41.660/680\par late on Wednesday. It ended at 41.420/460 to the dollar, after\par 41.270/310.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market for the third consecutive day on Thursday, starting to\par sell crowns at 41.600 per euro. It launched the action at around\par 41.120 on Tuesday, continuing within a wide range that at its\par weakest point hit around 41.900 crowns per euro on Wednesday.\par  Thursday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushed the crown briefly lower to\par around 41.800 per euro, but the unit recovered from the losses\par after the bank disappeared from the market, dealers said, adding\par they expected more firming pressure in the coming days.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "Interest in buying crowns has not weakened at all... We are\par closing stronger than we opened despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," one\par foreign bank trader said.\par     Dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would probably have to resort\par to cutting interest rates to make the crown less attractive for\par speculative capital pouring into central European markets in\par expectations of fat returns once the region joins the euro zone.\par     The bank's policy-making board will hold a regular bi-weekly\par meeting on Friday, but spokesman Jan Onda said interest rates\par were not on the agenda.\par     However, analysts and traders said expectations of a rate\par cut have been already priced into the market and added that such\par a move - a 25-to-50-basis-point cut is expected at a board\par meeting at the end of this month - would probably not quell\par crown buying.\par     "I think a cut below one percentage points would still make\par the crown a buy. A full one point cut would perhaps make it a\par buy one day later," the trader said.\par  \par     Slovakia's key two-week repo rate is currently 8.00 percent,\par following a 25 basis point cut at the end of October.\par     Slovak interest rates remain relatively high, compared to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par neighbouring Poland's key 28-day rate of 7.00 percent and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Czech two-week repo of 2.75 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021114dybe00wqx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
