\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak crown drops, then rebounds as cbank acts. \b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2002\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par  fell on Wednesday in the second straight day of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action to restrain it rise, but continued foreign buying\par allowed it to regain losses at the close of the session, dealers\par said.\par     The crown closed at 41.650/670 to the euro, its key\par reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , compared with 41.550/600 late on Tuesday,\par having climbed back from a one-month low of 41.880/900 early in\par the session.\par     It ended at 41.270/310 to the dollar, from 41.130/180.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it started to sell crowns at around\par 41.550 per euro after foreign buying pushed the unit stronger\par early on Wednesday, and it continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the level of\par around 41.900 crown per euro.\par     It was the second day in a row the bank moved against the\par surging crown. The bank declined to give volumes, but dealers\par estimated Wednesday's amount at around 150 million, after some\par 300 million euro bought by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     However, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appear to be only slowing down\par the firming trend as strong bullish sentiment on central\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  still prevails on the market, traders said.\par     "Once the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  disappeared from the market at around\par 41.900, the crown started to firm again," said Slovenska\par Sporitelna trader Juraj Zabadal.\par  \par     "I think they will have to do something again tomorrow,\par otherwise we will see another move stronger."\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are aimed at fighting off strong interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in EU candidate states' assets as foreign capital plays on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expectations of juicy yields once the applicant countries join\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the eurozone a few years after they enter the EU in around 2004.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts have said the Slovak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will probably\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par have to follow the example of its Czech counterpart, which has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par cut interest rates and undertaken months of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market, to fend off speculative capital inflow.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have repeatedly said they\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par would be ready to use all tools at the bank's disposal,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par including rate cuts, to prevent crown firming, which they deem\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unjustified due to Slovakia's wide trade deficit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dybd01ksj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
