\par\par \b \uc2  Dealers say  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  for second day. \b0\par\par\uc2 133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2002\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second day in a row on Wednesday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to weaken the crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders at local banks said. \par \par \uc2  "They started to sell crowns at around 41.470 per euro, and this pulled the crown to around 41.600," one local trader said. \par \par \uc2  The bank bought an estimated 250-300 million euros in aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, pulling the unit from 41.120 per euro to the current levels. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had repeatedly said the crown was overvalued following its rally sparked by a victory of pro-reform parties in September elections and fuelled by the convergence play tied to expectations of Slovakia's entry into the EU. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dybd00hx2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
