\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  crown drops after  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2002\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crown\par  fell by nearly one percent against the euro on Tuesday in\par volatile trade as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outweighed agressive\par euro buying by foreign players.\par     The crown, which hit an all-time high of 41.070/100 against\par the euro on Monday, was trading at 41.500/520 against its key\par reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by 1210 GMT. It was at 40.971/41.032 to the\par dollar from 40.710/760.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said earlier in the day it had started\par directly selling crowns for euro at around 41.120. The bank\par would not specify volumes, but dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had\par continued in several waves in gradually declining amounts.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still on the market. They started at\par 41.400, and they are bidding for one to two million euros at a\par time, much smaller than in the morning," said a dealer from a\par Bratislava-based bank, who wished to remain anonymous.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "They probably don't want to push the crown too much lower\par because so far they have always stopped at 41.500. It only seems\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is trying to drain all of the surplus liquidity\par from the market."\par     Dealers said the bank started its move to knock the unit\par lower by selling crowns for euros in two off-market deals with\par London brokers and then also sold euros on the automatic trading\par system in several waves.\par     But they added that bullish sentiment for the crown, due\par mainly to a win by centre-right forces in September elections\par and improved mood towards the region's markets as central Europe\par nears entry into the European Union, meant that foreign players\par would likely still buy crowns, leading to more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has repeatedly threatened to knock the\par crown lower in the past few weeks, claiming the unit's recent\par firming does not reflect the country's macroeconomic\par fundamentals.\par     Dealers said that unless the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took serious\par action, the unit could stay in a range of 41.200-500 in the near\par term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021112dybc00pb7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
