\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  crown shoots lower on cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2002\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crown\par  shot lower by nearly one percent at the open on Tuesday\par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively selling crowns,\par but it corrected slightly higher on buying from foreign players.\par     The crown was trading at 41.370/400 to its key reference\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro, from 41.140/170 late on Monday. It was at\par 40.939/999 to the dollar, from 40.710/760.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said earlier in the day it had started\par selling crowns for euros at around 41.120 near the open. It\par would not comment on volumes sold, but dealers said the amount\par may have been around 100 million euros worth of crowns.\par     Dealers said the bank sold crowns for euros in two\par off-market deals with London brokers and then also sold euros on\par the automatic trading system in at least two waves, the second\par coming at around 0900 GMT, knocking the crown lower to around\par 41.500 to the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "It seems that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was not happy with the\par crown's development, and it appeared they were willing to hold\par the crown below the 41.450 (per euro) level," said Tatra Banka\par dealer Boris Somorovsky.\par     "But they didn't succeed... Non-resident banks were\par interested in going long \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in interesting amounts (after\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )."\par     Another trader from an international bank in Bratislava\par added: "The second wave pushed the crown from below 41.400 to\par around 41.500."\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has repeatedly threatened to knock the\par crown lower in the past few weeks, as it says that recent\par firming on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is based solely on positive sentiment\par but does not reflect the country's macroeconomic fundamentals.\par     Dealers said that because the crown was not able to break\par the level of 41.500 to the euro, the unit could stay in a range\par of 41.200-500 in the nearest term, but it could go weaker if the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued aggressively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the crown had hit an all-time high\par of 41.070/100 at midday on Monday, up around eight percent since\par July on bullish sentiment tied to a win by rightist, pro-reform\par forces in September elections and the increased likelihood of\par Slovakia joining the EU and NATO in around 2004.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021112dybc00jge\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
