\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  to weaken crown. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Laca \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2002\par 16:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made good on recent threats and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock down the surging crown on Tuesday in what dealers said was likely the start of a long-lasting battle against the strong bullish mood on the unit. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold crowns in several waves to push the unit down from record highs where it had climbed along with other central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations of EU entry in 2004 and high rate differentials with western markets. \par \par \uc2  The crown had jumped by around five percent since September, when a pro-reform rightist coalition won parliamentary elections, sparking bullish sentiment and a strong inflow of foreign funds into the Slovak crown. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had repeatedly warned in recent weeks that the unit was overvalued because of Slovakia's wide foreign trade gap, and it finally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday to push it weaker to around 41.580/600 by the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "We started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the crown was at around 41.120 (to the euro)," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Treasury Director Frantisek Szulenyi told Reuters shortly after the session opened. \par \par \uc2  Szulenyi declined to detail volumes used in the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but market watchers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have sold between 200-250 million euros worth of crowns in a variety of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the day. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the effect of the move might not last for long, as foreign banks were still interested in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and more action could be needed to prevent it firming back in the direction of 41.070/100, the all-time high hit on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fail in what it is doing now," said Jan Toth, ING's chief economist in Bratislava. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this level help close some positions foreign investors have in the crown, but after some time investors will realise they want some Slovak exposure, so they will buy Slovak crowns...and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will appreciate." \par \par \uc2  LONDON STILL BULLISH ON REGION \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with off-market deals through London brokers just after the open, and then followed by more than two waves of crown selling on the automated trading system that London banks resisted with aggressive buying. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment in the region was also helped by ratings upgrades from Moody's Investors Service for Slovakia and seven other EU applicant countries due to economic restructuring in central Europe, but the markets' first reactions were muted. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the upgrades had been largely priced in, as foreign capital, noting the candidates were creeping closer to eurozone entry, had flooded bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with speculative convergence plays and cross deals. \par \par \uc2  "The Slovak crown has experienced a similar development as other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Hungarian forint, Polish zloty and the Czech crown, which have all been boosted by positive market sentiment," Commerzbank analyst Radomir Jac said. \par \par \uc2  "It can be expected that the positive mood towards the region will continue and the market will try to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger." \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have to cut interest rates to stem inflows into the crown due to their high differential with the West, despite an expected surge in 2003 inflation to be caused by utility price and tax hikes. \par \par \uc2  "The acceleration of inflation will be caused by administrative measures, rather than by demand pressure, so it will be outside of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  influence... There will be a certain room to lower the rates, and the room will widen in line with the pace of the crown firming," said Jac. \par \par \uc2  The key two-week repo rate is now 8.00 percent, following a 25-basis point cut at the end of October. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021112dybc00i80\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
