\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Slovak \highlight\uc2  cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again to boost crown. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Javurek \par \uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2002\par 15:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, June 18 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the third time in a week on Tuesday, boosting the crown by almost one percent and said it was ready to act again if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak unit has been under pressure since end-April due to concerns about the government overshooting its budget deficit target and uncertainty ahead of general elections in September. \par \par \uc2  The bank started to support the crown on June 11 with its first direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since January 2001, boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from around 44.800. \par \par \uc2  It did so again on Monday at 44.920 crowns to the euro, a three-year low and more than eight percent off near all-time highs hit in April, but Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still appeared to have surprised the market. \par \par \uc2  "We started at around 44.700. I will not say to what level we will continue," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Treasury Director Frantisek Szulenyi told Reuters shortly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bank had fought against the continuing downward draught by buying crowns until it managed to push the unit up to around 44.300. The crown traded at 44.430/470 by 1150 GMT, two hours after the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We keep watching (the market) and we're ready to act accordingly to developments," said Szulenyi. He declined to give a desired exchange rate level but earlier said 44.5 per euro was a "favoured level". \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also declined to say how much of its hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves it had spent to support the crown but dealers estimate the amount to be up to 150 million euros spread over the three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Szulenyi also rejected market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been defending the 45.000 level against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The bank has in the past said it has an internal range within which it wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move, saying the range could move according to economic performance, mainly in the external balance. \par \par \uc2  MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SEEN AHEAD \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the NBS would likely be forced to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight off the speculators as September's general elections approach. \par \par \uc2  "I think they will have to keep acting for maybe another week, if they really want to support the current levels and to beat the speculators. But I don't see it a huge problem, as the market volumes are low," said CSOB dealer Richard Brza. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers expect the crown will breach the psychological 45.000 support level ahead of the September 20-21 ballot. \par \par \uc2  The vote will be crucial to Slovakia's hopes to join the European Union and NATO. A victory by autocratic ex-Premier Vladimir Meciar, currently leading in the polls, could hinder Slovakia's integration chances. \par \par \uc2  American and EU officials have repeatedly warned that Meciar's return to power would jeopardise Slovakia's chances for entry into the two institutions. \par \par \uc2  Meciar was accused of backsliding on democratic and economic reforms while he was in power and Slovakia was overlooked when NATO expanded into post-Communist Europe in 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020618dy6i00fqz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
