\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Slovak cbank says will act if crown falls further. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Laca \par \uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 17:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA, June 6 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally on Thursday to prevent further falls in the edgy crown and said it would step directly into the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if its recent sharp decline went on. \par \par \uc2  Unlike other east European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have gained on foreign inflows linked to confidence in their joining the EU, the Slovak crown has weakened due to worries over widening budget and foreign trade gaps and political uncertainty. The crown's decline accelerated on Wednesday and Thursday, with the unit hitting 18-month lows. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marian Jusko's comments on Wednesday that he welcomed the recent shift to weakening from an uptrend earlier in the year sparked a sell-off. \par \par \uc2  The unit briefly rebounded on Thursday after the bank's treasurer said the market's reaction was overdone and that more depreciation would not be justified by economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "If this continues, and we see more daily falls of, say, one percent, then we will obviously react. But I will not say any concrete level," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Treasury Director Frantisek Szulenyi told Reuters in a brief telephone interview. \par \par \uc2  Market players said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon need to make good on its direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat if it wanted to hold the crown steady. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain nervous and prone to slide ahead of key September elections. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday Slovakia set September 20-21 as the date for the general election which will effectively decide if the country enters NATO and the European Union alongside its neighbours and if economic reform continues or stalls. \par \par \uc2  A return to power by authoritarian ex-premier Vladimir Meciar, whose party leads in the polls, would scupper Slovak chances of winning a NATO membership invitation at the Prague summit in November and leave the country isolated. \par \par \uc2  DOWNSIDE PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  The crown traded at around 44.43/47 to its key reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, shortly before Szulenyi's comments, compared with levels of 43.86/92 before Wednesday's statements by Jusko. \par \par \uc2  "The current level of the crown corresponds to economic fundamentals, and to the performance of the economy... I think the crown should now stabilise at around the present rates," Szulenyi said. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like we will go higher again (crown easing). The market might try to push it further, and we can soon test 44.50 (per euro)," said Slovenska Sporitelna trader Vladimir Gajdos. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign players would be tempted to try to find the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level by pushing the crown weaker. "The trend is clear. I don't think that anyone will come to this atmosphere and build crown positions," one foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The elections are coming, and the uncertainty is the reasons for buying (hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). We are in a completely different situation from the Czechs," he added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have wanted a weaker crown but it underestimated the market's sensitivity and may need to spend some reserves to prevent further decline. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody was expecting crown weakening before elections. But this is not the elections, this looks like a wish of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though executed in a little bit unfortunate way," said VUB bank analyst Pavol Popp. \par \par \uc2  A falling crown could help Slovakia's trade deficit, which was one of the reasons given for a rate hike in April. Some analysts also said that a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will mean more crown income from privatisation deals. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020606dy6600prz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
