\par\par \b \uc2  Slovak crown opens flat, may ease later. \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2002\par 10:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRATISLAVA, April 15 (Reuters) - The Slovak crown\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  was flat early on Monday, with dealers saying the unit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  might rather ease later in the day, on the expected slide of the \par \par \uc2  Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2      The Slovak crown, which has been driven by the rallying \par \par \uc2  Czech unit in the past few weeks, was at 41.43/48 to its key \par \par \uc2  reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, from 41.43/47 late on Friday. It \par \par \uc2  was at 47.01/06 to the dollar, from 47.10/14. \par \par \uc2      "We're closely watching the Czech (unit), which at the \par \par \uc2  moment trades just above the figure (30.00 to the euro). The \par \par \uc2  market tends to see it (the Czech crown) easing rather than \par \par \uc2  firming today. If so, I think we're going to move that direction \par \par \uc2  too," said ING dealer Marian Tatar. \par \par \uc2      However, Tatar said no trading could have been seen on the \par \par \uc2  Slovak market since the open. \par \par \uc2   "The market is pretty flat. There are some bids hanging at \par \par \uc2  around 41.45 (to the euro), but no movement was seen so far." \par \par \uc2      He added he saw the crown trading within a range of between \par \par \uc2  41.45 and 41.51 to the euro throughout the day. \par \par \uc2      Last week, the Slovak crown hit its all-time highs, driven \par \par \uc2  by the bullish Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but retreated from the levels on \par \par \uc2  Friday. \par \par \uc2      The crown briefly touched 41.33/37 to the euro early on \par \par \uc2  Friday, but some investors took profit from the crown's recent \par \par \uc2  uptrend, pushing it weaker at the close of the day. \par \par \uc2      The Slovak crown, which has gained over three percent this \par \par \uc2  year, has followed the neighbouring Czech crown as some \par \par \uc2  investors play the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  together and others hedge \par \par \uc2  their euro exposure through the Czech/\b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair. \par \par \uc2  The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in as many weeks to \par \par \uc2  knock its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, but it has so far failed to weaken it \par \par \uc2  substantially from record highs of around 30.0 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  Dealers do not expect the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step into \par \par \uc2  the market to weaken the crown soon, with some saying the market \par \par \uc2  will watch 41.00-per-euro as a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020415dy4f00has\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
