\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Venezuelan bolivar steady on Chavez ousting. \b0\par\par\uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2002\par 11:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 12 (Reuters) - The Venezuelan bolivar showed held steady in Europe after Friday's confirmation that president Hugo Chavez had resigned, having jumped against the dollar on Thursday as the pressure on him mounted. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Czech crown fell from record high levels against the euro following verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, while the Turkish lira hit new nine-month highs versus the dollar on growing optimism on the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  The bond market cheered Chavez's departure but dealers say it may not be an unequivocal positive for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  given it is also likely to bring oil prices down, one of Venezuela's main revenue sources. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar hit two-month highs against the dollar on Thursday as another mass anti-Chavez demonstration upped the pressure on the unpopular president. \par \par \uc2  Venezuela's armed forces chief Lucas Rincon appeared on national television on Friday to confirm that Chavez had resigned following the demonstration in Caracas, in which at least 10 people died and over 80 were injured. \par \par \uc2  "There's almost a sense of euphoria in the markets, with the view taking hold that Venezuela is now back in the real world and can move forward," said Peter West, chief Latin American economist at BBVA Securities in London. \par \par \uc2  "However, once that euphoria dies down we are likely to enter a period of reflection and it's far from clear what comes next," he added. \par \par \uc2  One of the main factors keeping Chavez in office was the lack of a clear alternative and analysts said that a period of uncertainty was now likely to ensue until elections can be held. \par \par \uc2  Pedro Carmona, the head of Venezuela's leading business association, Fedecamaras, said he would head a transition government that would lead to free elections. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in London said there had been little trade in the bolivar despite the news, the unit is sparsely traded in Europe. \par \par \uc2  The recent rally in crude prices had been inspired in part by a six-week oil workers' strike in Venezuela, and prices immediately came under pressure as news of Chavez's ousting emerged. \par \par \uc2  At 0925 GMT the bolivar traded at 850 to the dollar . \par \par \uc2  VERBAL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  DENTS CROWN \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown was brought down from earlier record highs below 30 to the euro as Czech National Bank governor Zdenek Tuma pledged to continue efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The crown eased as the market latched on to an opinion article in the business daily Hospodarske Noviny, in which Tuma said the bank's policy would aim to deflate what he called an "exchange rate bubble" that could be dangerous for the country's economic stability. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers increasingly see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as losing credibility in its efforts to slow down the crown's appreciation, with two \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a week failing to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting fresh record highs. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is out the picture. They say there is no fundamental reason for the crown to be here, but if you look at the amount of portfolio inflows there is a every reason," said one London dealer. \par \par \uc2  "I would say there are good fundamental reasons why people want to buy Czech companies," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said the market was probably now getting over-stretched on the upside and that players would be looking to take profits. However, he added that any weakness was likely to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The crown traded at 30.11 to the euro from 30.04 and at 34.195 to the dollar from 33.97. The Slovak crown followed its Czech counterpart to record highs against the euro around early on, before being pegged back. \par \par \uc2  Strong foreign investment inflows are also buoying the Slovak unit, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been more relaxed about its rise. \par \par \uc2  Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Treasury Director Frantisek Szulenyi told Reuters on Thursday that the bank had no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown traded at 41.442 to the euro from 41.42 and at 47.08 to the dollar from 46.94. \par \par \uc2  The Turkish lira extended its rally up to nine-month peaks against the dollar on growing optimism over the domestic economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  An upbeat \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey on Thursday raised hopes that an annual inflation target of 35 percent for 2002 would be met, and boosted hopes for further rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  The lira traded at 1,282,500 to the dollar on the interbank market from 1,292,000 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020412dy4c00jx2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
